## Level 6 completed

- `pthm-rpm-h-coordinate-versus-observer-energy`: Confirmed refuter:25:2. For N=1,h=δ,β=(0,sin(t/t0),0), H depends on t off p2=0, but Hamilton evolution with p=(p1,0,0) has constant p and constant H. The corrected statement makes time independence sufficient; dH/dt=H_t gives conservation exactly when H_t vanishes along that solution interval. Pairing with the future normal gives E_n=cκ>0; differentiation gives w=h-inverse p/κ and its massive/null norm bounds. Positive h bounds β·p and proves H>0 for |β|<N; aligned large p proves negativity possible for |β|>N. At |β|=N the statement makes no additional strict-positivity claim. SR is used only in the flat specialization; no observed inputs occur.
- `pthm-rpm-h-massive-and-null-time-motion`: E²=m²c⁴+c²|p|² with positive E gives |qdot|<c when m>0, including p=0, and |qdot|=c for the separately assumed m=0,p≠0 branch. Reduced equations give pdot=0. The conclusions are conditional on the respective free actions, with no measured speed or empirical precision claimed.
- `ex-rpm-h-shifted-chart-negative-energy`: The explicit invertible linear transformation pulls the Minkowski form back to the shift-2 metric. N=1,h=δ gives temporal dx0 even though ∂0 is spacelike. p1>mc/√3 implies κ<2p1 and H<0, while p(n)=−κ and E_n>0. Since m>0, 0<p1/κ<1, so |qdot|/c=2−p1/κ>1 and observer speed is c p1/κ<c. The written extra lower bound p1/κ>1/2 is unnecessary; the preceding upper bound supplies the inequality immediately. No empirical result is asserted.

